The drawing is the transverse section of a false fruit such as the apple, where the fleshy edible part develops from the **thalamus** and not from the ovary. The labels read:
- the wide outer fleshy zone (P) is the **thalamus**;
- the bodies inside the central chambers (Q) are the **seeds**;
- the papery lining of the chambers (R) is the **endocarp**;
- the region just outside the chambers (S) is the **mesocarp**.

So the answer is option (B) P – Thalamus, Q – Seed, R – Endocarp, S – Mesocarp.

DNA is a hydrophilic molecule and cannot pass through cell membranes, so the bacterial cells must first be made **competent** to take up DNA:
- the bacterial cells are treated with a specific concentration of a **divalent cation**, such as calcium (\(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)), which increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall;
- the cells are then incubated with the recombinant DNA **on ice**;
- this is followed by placing them briefly at **42\(^{\circ}\)C** (heat shock);
- and then putting them **back on ice**.

This enables the bacteria to take up the recombinant DNA.

The replicating fork is drawn as a Y:
- the two **parental (template) strands** separate at the fork, one running 5' to 3' and the other 3' to 5', with each end labelled;
- on the template with **3' to 5' polarity**, the new strand is made as one long arrow pointing towards the fork: **continuous synthesis**;
- on the template with **5' to 3' polarity**, the new strand is made as short arrows pointing away from the fork: **discontinuous synthesis** (Okazaki fragments, later joined by DNA ligase);
- both **newly synthesised strands** grow in the 5' to 3' direction, and their 5' and 3' ends are marked.

DNA replication occurs within such a small opening because DNA is a very long molecule, and the two strands cannot be separated along their entire length at once owing to the **very high energy requirement**. So replication starts at the origin and proceeds through a small opening of the DNA helix, the replication fork.
